# P00.3 R11 live proof — glm-cli

Two real, live `dispatch execute` attempts against the real
`.fgos/config.json` in this repo. Same bounded read-only inline task as the
codex-cli proof (read `package.json`, report the top-level `name` field, no
mutation).

## Attempt 0 — config-tier stop (expected, not a bug)

Command:
```
node src/runner/dispatch.mjs execute --contract <contract-v2.json> --executor glm-cli --cwd /home/vantt/projects/forgentX
```
Exit code: 1. stderr:
```
no model configured for policy tier "standard" under provider "z-ai".
```
`.fgos/config.json`'s `runner.modelPolicies.z-ai` only maps `lightweight`
(`"z-ai": {"lightweight": "z-ai/glm-5.2"}`). An inline `--contract` carries
no `assignment.policy` at all (confirmed:
`test/runner/assignment-provenance.test.mjs`'s inline-no-domain test asserts
`assignment.policy === undefined`), so `resolveAssignmentDispatchPolicy`'s
tier floor defaults to `'standard'` (`assignment-policy.mjs` line 81). Tier
resolution is explicitly monotonic — `--tier lightweight` on the CLI cannot
lower a `'standard'` floor (`assignment-policy.mjs` lines 96-104, "cannot be
weakened" per the module's own docstring), confirmed by retrying with
`--tier lightweight` and getting the identical error. This is R8's
`supportsPolicyTier` gate working exactly as designed — a real, correct stop
condition of the existing system, not an executor-availability failure and
not a CLI-wiring defect.

**Resolution used for the actual live attempt below:** switched to the
`--assignment` door with a hand-built declared Assignment
(`buildAssignment({..., policy: { minTier: 'lightweight' } })`) — `policy`
is a documented, supported caller override on `buildAssignment` (see its
JSDoc: "Optional caller-supplied policy overrides"), which sets the tier
floor to `'lightweight'` from the Assignment's own declared policy (not a
CLI override), letting `z-ai`'s only configured tier resolve legally.

## Attempt 1 — real spawn, killed by outer harness timeout (inconclusive, not counted)

```
timeout 180 node src/runner/dispatch.mjs execute --assignment asgn_tsk_p003_glm_live_proof_validate_plan_001 --executor glm-cli --cwd /home/vantt/projects/forgentX
```
The surrounding shell tool itself enforced a 120s cap and killed the whole
command before the inner `timeout 180` could fire on its own. Real stderr
was captured up to the kill point:
```
fgos: dispatch capability=(none declared) executor=glm-cli via=cli-spawn provider=claude model=z-ai/glm-5.2 tier=lightweight
"z-ai/glm-5.2" is not a model this version of Claude Code recognizes, so auto-compact will keep this session within 200k tokens ...
[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {"model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","query_source":"sdk"}
```
Not counted as the real attempt (harness artifact, not the executor's own
behavior) — retried with the shell tool given a longer outer budget.

## Attempt 2 — real spawn, genuine timeout (the bounded retry; counted)

```
timeout 180 node src/runner/dispatch.mjs execute --assignment asgn_tsk_p003_glm_live_proof_validate_plan_001 --executor glm-cli --cwd /home/vantt/projects/forgentX
```
Exit code: 124 (the inner `timeout 180` itself fired). stderr, in full:
```
fgos: dispatch capability=(none declared) executor=glm-cli via=cli-spawn provider=claude model=z-ai/glm-5.2 tier=lightweight
"z-ai/glm-5.2" is not a model this version of Claude Code recognizes, so auto-compact will keep this session within 200k tokens (the context window it assumes). If the model accepts more, append [1m] to the model name for 1M, or set CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS to its real window; to make it recognized, map it in the modelOverrides setting or update Claude Code; CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_UNKNOWN_MODEL_WINDOW_ENFORCEMENT=1 restores the previous wait-for-the-API behavior.
[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {"model":"z-ai/glm-5.2","query_source":"sdk"}
```
No further stdout ever arrived — the underlying `claude -p` process (glm-cli
is a Claude Code CLI invocation routed to OpenRouter via
`ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`/`ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN` env substitution, per
`.fgos/config.json`'s glm-cli executor entry) genuinely hung past the 180s
bound after printing the "unrecognized_model" warning, with no visible
response from the OpenRouter-routed GLM model. `timeout 180` sent SIGTERM to
its direct child (`node dispatch.mjs`); the grandchild `claude -p ...`
process (PID 2227996, confirmed via `ps`) was NOT part of that process
group and survived as an orphan — found and cleaned up manually
(`kill -TERM 2227996`) after this attempt, confirmed gone.

**This is a real, observed live-executor timeout for `glm-cli`** — the same
category of failure the coordinator's brief pre-identified as a documented
baseline pattern (index.md's Baseline table, item #8: codex-cli timing out
at 120000ms is the precedent this failure mode was already known for; here
it recurs for glm-cli specifically). Per the plan's Autonomous Execution
Contract #4, this is a stop gate, not permission to fabricate a passing
result.

## What DID get proven for glm-cli despite the timeout

The dispatch-plan for this attempt (both attempt 1 and attempt 2; identical)
was written before the executor spawned and shows correct, accurate
resolution — this is real evidence, not fabricated:
```
.fgos/assignments/asgn_tsk_p003_glm_live_proof_validate_plan_001/runs/02/dispatch-plan.json:
{"selector":{"type":"assignment","value":"asgn_tsk_p003_glm_live_proof_validate_plan_001"},
 "mechanism":"out-of-process","executorId":"glm-cli","capability":"glm-cli",
 "invocation":{"via":"cli","adapter":"cli-spawn","protocol":"prompt-stdout-v1"},
 "governance":{"providerFamily":"z-ai",
   "egress":{"kind":"cross-provider","target":"https://openrouter.ai/api","content":"repo-content"}},
 "reasonCodes":["native-first.0033.cli-spawn-shaped"],"configured":true}
```
`executorId: "glm-cli"` (from `--executor glm-cli` reaching
`cliOverride.preferExecutor` correctly on the `--assignment` door),
`governance.providerFamily: "z-ai"` and `egress.target:
"https://openrouter.ai/api"` both match `.fgos/config.json`'s glm-cli entry
exactly (`providerModel: "z-ai"`, `env.ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL:
"https://openrouter.ai/api"`). The stderr banner line
(`provider=claude model=z-ai/glm-5.2 tier=lightweight`) also confirms the
resolved model string (`z-ai/glm-5.2`, from `modelPolicies.z-ai.lightweight`)
was passed through correctly to the real `claude -p ... --model z-ai/glm-5.2`
invocation. `.fgos/secrets.local.env`'s `GLM_OPENROUTER_API_KEY` was
substituted internally by the existing `${VAR}` env-substitution convention
in `transport.mjs` (never read or printed directly by this proof).

**Verdict: R11's glm-cli half is BLOCKED by a genuine live-executor timeout,
confirmed on one bounded retry (two real spawn attempts, one killed
prematurely by the harness, one hit its own 180s bound with no response).**
R9 (`--executor` CLI wiring on both doors) and R10 (`feature.yaml` fallback
cleanup) are unaffected and fully proven — this dispatch-plan evidence alone
already confirms `--executor glm-cli` correctly reached
`cliOverride.preferExecutor` on the `--assignment` door and resolved
accurate provider/governance provenance before the executor's own runtime
hung.
